00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.820 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  Razors new blade is a beautiful and  powerful gaming laptop and while it's  similar to last year's model in many  ways it's also $400 more expensive for a  product that's already pricier than the  competition that price increase raises  some alarms as you'd expect the hardware  on the inside is stronger and more  capable at gaming under higher settings  but it's the new display that stands out  as the most notable upgrade it's  extremely sharp and doubles as a touch  panel but does that justify the extra  cost let's take a look if you put razors  new blade next to last year's model you  might have a hard time telling the two  apart the only real difference being  that the new blade is slightly thicker  at 0.7 inches which is still  impressively thin considering what's on  the inside at 4.7 pounds it's not  unusually light but it's on the better  end of the gaming laptop scale razors  bright green accents the logo on the lid  do stand out but the blades largely  understated and pretty sleek which isn't  something you can say about most gaming  laptops the black anodized aluminum  chassis looks great but it comes with  one downside it transfers heat so well  that it's impossible to keep the blade  on your lap ball gaming or watching HD  videos is it a trade-off yes but it's  not a deal-breaker apart from the GPU  this year's blade is nearly identical on  the inside to last year's model it has  the same processor a quad core intel  core i7 4702 HQ at 2.2 gigahertz in the  same RAM configuration with 8 gigabytes  of 1600 megahertz ddr3 memory for the  new GPU Razer has gone for the fresh  Nvidia 870 M with three gigabytes of  gddr5 vram onboard the 870 M outperforms  last year's 765 M in every case but this  added performance comes at a cost  the 875 M draws 100 watts of power where  last year's 765 only needed 75 watts and  given that razors using the same battery  as last year the new blade won't last  long on the go when you push the GPU to  its limits the blade will typically last  just over an hour in our experience  although this can be extended a bit with  conservative graphics settings otherwise  expect three to four hours of battery  life during non gaming sessions  the display on last year's blade had a  maximum resolution of 1600 by 900 pixels  given the physical dimensions it was  adequate but not impressive this time  around the opposite is true  razor went all out and opted for a 3200  by 1800 qhd+ display with 10-point  multi-touch capabilities it's a  magnificent screen that produces vibrant  colors in any light and its contents for  me invisible from every angle shy of 90  degrees the blade screen is impressive  on many levels but art specs necessary  for a gaming laptop a 1080p or 1440p  display would have been perfectly  acceptable and it's not really clear why  a gaming laptop needs a touch panel yes  it's a display that's easy to appreciate  but it's over-the-top features come at a  significant cost there's also the fact  that you likely won't play any of your  games at the blades native resolution  given that it would be far too taxing on  any gaming PC if you're aiming for  decent graphics settings let alone the  blade as such that means you'll have to  deal with some minor inconveniences for  one some applications including Origen  don't scale correctly but more  importantly when you try to play a game  at 1080p you're treated to a small  in-game display in the middle of a large  black screen the solution here is pretty  simple set the desktop resolution to  match the game you're going to play you  could always leave the desktop  resolution at 1920 by 1080 to avoid this  hassle but then everything in Windows is  slightly larger than it should be  there's nothing worse than a laptop with  a poorly designed keyboard and a finicky  touch but these are things you'd never  say about the blade the keyboards  chiclet style keys feel great and  they're perfectly spaced it's easy to  draw comparisons to the keys on a  MacBook Pro which is a good sign the  touchpad is also worthy of praise with a  smooth and responsive surface that works  well with Windows 8 multi-touch gestures  presenting no issues whatsoever during  our tests the buttons below the touchpad  aren't as pleasing as the rest of the  inputs being thin and slightly mushy but  they still get the job done looking at  the inputs on the side of the blade  nothing's changed since last year on the  left side you've got the power supply  connection two USB 3.0 ports and a 3.5  millimeter microphone headphone combo  port and on the other an HDMI port and  one more USB 3.0 connection it's a shame  that there's not an Ethernet port but  there's built-in 802 1011 AVGN EC Wi-Fi  and support for Bluetooth 4.0 if you  want more reliable and faster internet  access you'll have to shell out for an  Ethernet USB adapter for a thin gaming  laptop the blade handles demanding games  including watchdogs Admiral  you're best off aiming for high or very  high graphic settings rather than ultra  but that's if you're chasing 60 frames  per second if you're okay with 30 frames  per second you can eke out a little more  performance from the blades GPU and CPU  in most cases you can find other laptops  that offer better performance for less  money but you ultimately sacrifice  Portability and build well the  importance of which should not be  underestimated that aside you won't be  disappointed by the blades performance  you won't be able to play every game  with ultra settings but you can get  close in most cases the blade that razor  supplied for our review is the 256  gigabyte model which costs around $2,400  before taxes the base model with 128  gigabyte solid state drive gets off the  ground at 2,200 dollars and the high-end  model with 512 gigabytes cost about  $2,700 the blade is definitely a premium  high-end laptop and it's easy to balk at  the price tag but when the blades in  your hands it's really hard not to fall  in love with its sleek portable and  powerful enough to keep any PC gamer on  the go in a good mood any model of the  blade is a really nice laptop and if  budgets are concerned don't discount  last year's model which is currently on  sale for $1,700 but if you've got money  to spend there's no other gaming laptop  on the market that I would recommend  more than razors new blade  [Music]  you

